In article <8045@star.cs.vu.nl> lbkruij@cs.vu.nl (=Kruijswijk Lucas B) writes:
>In article <HEBRAIS.90Oct25032005@degusse.IRO.UMontreal.CA> hebrais@degusse.IRO.UMontreal.CA (Philippe Hebrais) writes:
>>Having a human called Santa-Claus walking around level 1 giving (small)
>>gifts to all lawful monsters and characters on christmas day.

>Good idea! But not only American folklore, please! I have never had any gifts
>from Santa-Clause, but many from St. Nicolas. Santa-Clause is less popular
>in Holland than in America.

True.  We do need to think of those who are not familiar with American
folklore.

The name Santa Claus comes from the Dutch settlers of New York, 
(Originally New Amsterdam) as Saint Nicholas appeared to the ears of the
English.    It's really "Sa'nt  'a'c'laus"

By the way, I like the holiday idea.  Let's be fair to chaotic characters
though.  On halloween, a demon appears and gives them three wishes?
I suppose Neutral characters can get something special for Labor Day.
A knight get's something for Veteran's and Memorial Days.  Well, these
are all American holidays, so perhaps this wouldn't fit.

Valkyries might get something special for certain days of the week,
Wednesday (Wodin's day) a gift from (W)odin, Thursday (Thor's day)
a gift from Thor (maybe Mjollnir!), Friday (Freia's (sp?) day) a gift
from that goddess.

Our holidays and days of the week are very mythological really.
Even our months.  (eg. April derives from "Avril" which means, "boar"
and refers to the death of Adonis (Greek/Roman  mythology) which 
happened during this month.  January refers to god of beginnings, Janus)

An element like this would fit very well in the fantasy context of nethack.
